  "full_text": " **But wait, 10 cents seems too cheap for a baseball ball. Maybe I made a mistake.**\n\n**Solution:** Let the ball cost \\( b \\) dollars. Then \\( 2b + 1 \\) is the cost of the bat. Together, they equal \\$1.10. So, \\( 2b + 1 = 1.10 \\), \\( 2b = 0.10 \\), \\( b = 0.05 \\). So, the ball costs 5 cents.\n\nWait, 5 cents seems suspicious. Maybe I need to rethink this.\n\n**Solution:** Let me try another approach. Suppose the ball costs \\( x \\) dollars. Then the bat costs \\( x + 1 \\) dollars. Together, \\( x + (x + 1) = 1.10 \\). So, \\( 2x + 1 = 1.10 \\), which gives \\( 2x = 0.10 \\), so \\( x = 0.05 \\). So, the ball is 5 cents, the bat is 11 cents. That adds up: 5 + 11 = 16"
}